f-43b7-5f1c-a891-d39157eb162b" :question/math-academy-id "q-84750" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of members of a school band\n- The number of goals scored by a soccer player in $10$ penalties if the probability of scoring any penalty is $65%$\n- The number of victories of a chess player in $5$ matches if the probability of the player winning any individual game is $45%$" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of school band members cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of goals in $10$ penalties can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $5$ matches can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $5$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-84750/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84750"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-84043"] :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The weights of $30$ professional wrestlers\n- The number of times that the number $6$ appears when a fair die is rolled $20$ times\n- The price of milk at the store" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The weights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of times that the number $6$ appears when a die is rolled $20$ times can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $20$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The prices cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-89355/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "cd0e89a9-d5f4-53f1-b7b6-d01695f81a59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89355/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "5bae84a9-7824-5533-b448-7bb2a373cca9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89355/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "9129e91a-9091-522f-80d8-de3794b1435b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89355/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "a3f610a3-bd6e-5695-868f-3c0b583dc676" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89355/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "a11b61c8-bfbf-52e4-ab2e-6aa9ece0dce3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensu