ficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-89355/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-89355"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130376/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "659859e4-f7dd-5be4-ada0-a95ec768b357" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130376/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "4e5a6a19-848b-57f5-8875-9ac54708c707" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130376/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "61d3f12e-1b75-5e74-bd73-714069ac429c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130376/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "db434826-72e1-5b0b-a64e-cd8f681ca588" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130376/selection/f8bc450b7f4fe2953896918c1f5c69e16edb279ae4186289918e142ef04a634f" :answer/id #uuid "a799cce2-c14f-51a2-87e1-c6e3f278cabc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II  only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130376/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b0e1759-9ae0-5f7a-8c53-6d26ca6e0d18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-130376/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-130376/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-130376/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-130376/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-130376/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-130376/selection/f8bc450b7f4fe2953896918c1f5c69e16edb279ae4186289918e142ef04a634f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130376" :question/id #uuid "00947d44-f147-5655-8d88-1b3aaa7c544b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130376" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of students who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected primary school children\n- The number of three-point shots made by a basketball player in 15 shots\n- The number of purchases made with a credit card in a store out of $10$ observed purchases, if, on average, $75%$ of all purchases are made with a credit card" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of children who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected students can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $200$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of three-point shots in $15$ shots can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $15$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of purchases made with a credit card in $10$ purchases can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-130376/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130376"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-89354/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e0103aff-aa8c-5935-8572-a9482961e2e4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89354/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c320b944-bc67-5c83-a755-a310061b83ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89354/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "63f1d21e-7265-54b1-846a-ed1a4b870c9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89354/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "58cec676-a0c3-5f27-bd87-fb55dfe6c983" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89354/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b42a8f8a-63db-5295-9d03-1fa75bd64a5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89354/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b08a43f6-fdc6-5a1f-a83f-7fb7d47352c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-89354/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-89354/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-89354/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-89354/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-89354/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-89354/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-89354" :question/id #uuid "8067fad5-eb5e-5c22-891f-93bc6eeb8202" :question/math-academy-id "q-89354" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of baskets made by a basketball player attempting three free throws if the probability of scoring any free throw is $65%$\n- The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items produced by a machine\n- The number of views of a video on a popular video channel" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of baskets in $3$ throws can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $3$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $100$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of views cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-89354/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-89354"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331462/field-1/c84237ccce73dfda231a8e7162bbcf92ffb93e40dd46cc106a9d6f5adc1534bb" :answer/id #uuid "5bd97b00-15bf-5928-9ee6-12974585feed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.346" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331462/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8bd1f4e-932f-5edf-88fc-92e4298c4bf5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331462/field-1/c84237ccce73dfda231a8e7162bbcf92ffb93e40dd46cc106a9d6f5adc1534bb" :answer-field/choices ["q-331462/field-1/c84237ccce73dfda231a8e7162bbcf92ffb93e40dd46cc106a9d6f5adc1534bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331462" :question/id #uuid "9e9e0d94-9420-56a4-b27d-db080d52e145" :question/math-academy-id "q-331462" :question/problem "A biased spinner has a $60%$ chance of landing on blue. The spinner is spun $4$ times. Rounded to three decimal places, what is the probability of getting blue exactly $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner landed on blue.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=4$ spins.\n\n- The probability of landing on blue is $p=60%=0.6.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(4,0.6).$ We want to find the probability of landing on blue $x=2$ times.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})(0.6)^{2}(1-0.6)^{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!}(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =6(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.346,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-331462/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331462"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331494/field-1/17efb07d56390f6d21c2a8d68e49fe0a8cb4f97dbfdf90c0da49b9a9e64e6e61" :answer/id #uuid "0fc018de-b4c8-51b5-bf01-170c762a2ea3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.297" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331494/field-1/48fb4afd70a5a8d8c5c01a602c7c81d88cf62dda82c22c4c69cdfef2b24d1b04" :answer/id #uuid "415ba8b1-2d29-535e-8828-c6ed1bca43b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.296" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331494/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1633915a-7e35-5ab8-9eb1-031b5fc043dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331494/field-1/17efb07d56390f6d21c2a8d68e49fe0a8cb4f97dbfdf90c0da49b9a9e64e6e61" :answer-field/choices ["q-331494/field-1/17efb07d56390f6d21c2a8d68e49fe0a8cb4f97dbfdf90c0da49b9a9e64e6e61" "q-331494/field-1/48fb4afd70a5a8d8c5c01a602c7c81d88cf62dda82c22c4c69cdfef2b24d1b04"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331494" :question/id #uuid "ae728baf-c8f8-57a9-b721-7ee1b58bb5b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-331494" :question/problem "A fair eight-sided die is rolled $6$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting a $7$ or $8$ exactly two times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times that a $7$ or $8$ is rolled. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=6$ rolls.\n\n- The probability of getting a $7$ or $8$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,\\frac{1}{4}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=2$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})}^{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{2!(6-2)!}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{4} \\\\  & =15{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.297,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331494/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331494"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-276118/field-1/d094ab9a6e8cb1a171560cebe8fe4c9098895b4bc4a0504201a141eb8f1c092d" :answer/id #uuid "e197b6ca-1439-519f-afbc-aff2c2543b83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.161" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-276118/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f223500-1966-5321-9a91-57851edf3334" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-276118/field-1/d094ab9a6e8cb1a171560cebe8fe4c9098895b4bc4a0504201a141eb8f1c092d" :answer-field/choices ["q-276118/field-1/d094ab9a6e8cb1a171560cebe8fe4c9098895b4bc4a0504201a141eb8f1c092d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-276118" :question/id #uuid "a60095c2-6da0-5e01-9dc8-1c655b3bea85" :question/math-academy-id "q-276118" :question/problem "A fair six-sided die is rolled $5$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting a $6$ exactly two times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times that a $6$ is rolled. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ rolls.\n\n- The probability of getting a $6$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{6}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=2$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})}^{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!}{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3} \\\\  & =10{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.161,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-276118/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-276118"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-89366/selection/728ab3b7cbf95e451db964e067098bab6cb3bb6b43b52b00c22261cfb3fe8e14" :answer/id #uuid "5dbeff02-7e8c-5746-abfb-4828d504e26e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.089" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89366/selection/73654ae7bede92a6fb37c4e84228445235305b01af7f7219f6c2db8a2d6b529d" :answer/id #uuid "130e6a0f-26d9-561c-822d-7174a582fbe8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.158" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89366/selection/723b24f6529fb868faa9b9e23c8e822d12243f73cbb9f0d6529a0016721f76eb" :answer/id #uuid "5f9155f9-c8dc-5039-a061-b9d44b58b0fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.101" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89366/selection/f52a86de3cd55a1a00d21804b115fc791013e405345231e33a7790949a391cee" :answer/id #uuid "213ce3c4-0fd4-5e42-9487-ec1a9d9c3f84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.115" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89366/selection/3c55d96ccb385c3e5f702b10f1905433d9ed1b4105ab24f070f2808a72bd0c03" :answer/id #uuid "57dfb6e7-4820-5386-aca0-6d10105db075" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.136" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89366/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "21db5908-14a7-57fd-9656-f7706596e1ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-89366/selection/723b24f6529fb868faa9b9e23c8e822d12243f73cbb9f0d6529a0016721f76eb" :answer-field/choices ["q-89366/selection/728ab3b7cbf95e451db964e067098bab6cb3bb6b43b52b00c22261cfb3fe8e14" "q-89366/selection/73654ae7bede92a6fb37c4e84228445235305b01af7f7219f6c2db8a2d6b529d" "q-89366/selection/723b24f6529fb868faa9b9e23c8e822d12243f73cbb9f0d6529a0016721f76eb" "q-89366/selection/f52a86de3cd55a1a00d21804b115fc791013e405345231e33a7790949a391cee" "q-89366/selection/3c55d96ccb385c3e5f702b10f1905433d9ed1b4105ab24f070f2808a72bd0c03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-89366" :question/id #uuid "ed2637f8-1525-514c-9c1e-97be8eb8d1c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89366" :question/problem "A spinner with three regions is designed so that the probability it lands on red is $\\frac{3}{10},$ the probability it lands on blue is $\\frac{2}{5},$ and on green $\\frac{3}{10}.$ What is the probability that the spinner lands on red or green exactly $5$ times if it is spun $12$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner lands on red or green.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ spins\n\n- the probability of landing on red or green on any spin is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,\\frac{3}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=5$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{5}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})}^{12-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{5!(12-5)!}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{7} \\\\  & =792{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{7} \\\\  & =0.101\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-89366/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-89366"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130383/selection/446d1dea440f49dde59fcefd97e4d8a4ed9dd5e14603741246fa23c5f5005864" :answer/id #uuid "9163f413-bf9b-51cd-89ea-175faa3941de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.071" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130383/selection/98dd22aca43b11b151665f22ed911567947d8dcbadcd8083f07aeaab55fa76c0" :answer/id #uuid "82b67541-f209-59b4-8d80-7ff571ffb443" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.263" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130383/selection/5e0579e6f0ca6d23dc2104f7dd565cb4109a26068b3a19f124dad91e780d2c02" :answer/id #uuid "ee5cbb3b-fcf5-533c-974e-29d585b3433d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.257" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130383/selection/0b655060625fa7cdca393844b07e6309896190bd10320208461f3ede3ab3587e" :answer/id #uuid "1d1a17ae-cb25-5faa-8bfd-4d271840ce35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.157" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130383/selection/7a858b239b2bdc0a1c63f173a7204e7f46afabf693bff062de10b802a8844040" :answer/id #uuid "17db83ee-84fa-5e14-808a-29bee9743282" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.172" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130383/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6049ed22-210f-5012-ba93-8d31f5a47e6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-130383/selection/0b655060625fa7cdca393844b07e6309896190bd10320208461f3ede3ab3587e" :answer-field/choices ["q-130383/selection/446d1dea440f49dde59fcefd97e4d8a4ed9dd5e14603741246fa23c5f5005864" "q-130383/selection/98dd22aca43b11b151665f22ed911567947d8dcbadcd8083f07aeaab55fa76c0" "q-130383/selection/5e0579e6f0ca6d23dc2104f7dd565cb4109a26068b3a19f124dad91e780d2c02" "q-130383/selection/0b655060625fa7cdca393844b07e6309896190bd10320208461f3ede3ab3587e" "q-130383/selection/7a858b239b2bdc0a1c63f173a7204e7f46afabf693bff062de10b802a8844040"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130383" :question/id #uuid "1db231fa-25cb-50bf-aeea-fb3a82c53299" :question/math-academy-id "q-130383" :question/problem "The probability that John succeeds in selling an insurance policy to a given customer is $0.45.$ On a given day, he interacts with $8$ customers. Assuming that the transactions with each customer are independent, what is the probability that he will sell exactly $2$ insurance policies on this day? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of insurance policies that John sells on this day.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ customers\n\n- the probability that John succeeds in selling an insurance policy to a given customer is $p=0.45$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.45).$ We want to find the probability that he will sell exactly $2$ insurance policies.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2})(0.45)^{2}(1-0.45)^{8-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8!}{2!(8-2)!}(0.45)^{2}(0.55)^{6} \\\\  & =28(0.45)^{2}(0.55)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.157\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-130383/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130383"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130573/selection/35ff00cbcf2a4a436b128a72e6f455ba8059d75be15bd5630c813d42a056329a" :answer/id #uuid "ca29e2d6-ebde-5a30-a0ee-e0f9efbb64fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0107" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130573/selection/2e2fe98a36cbe7c66ae79581c1fdd75b0f5c7f4a981eacf24dd5f5f8809f4122" :answer/id #uuid "7d847e63-f8e8-5ac0-a2fd-59ec3f5b53f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2440" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130573/selection/fe6d27559e5abcde73055ea85de7c50227a6e0dceca7459f59df25f54c346eaa" :answer/id #uuid "34eab8e0-d2d5-5b0c-a39c-961f23811b95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5256" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130573/selection/6585fa59d5b14ce27b0bb7d1a2bb77f02b12ab36e5f9d7e4e03a487b2988d09f" :answer/id #uuid "26ce30a9-15e3-575d-80b2-603399220c2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0004" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130573/selection/90f58f6c83fbc4be8de7c71ec4260b5b9f59480107489cc2ec1a33687cc9f2a1" :answer/id #uuid "f6c827cd-1b40-5747-bdab-8ceb1903f9b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0547" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130573/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd533fb-651e-5041-b53b-7a326b772a62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-130573/selection/6585fa59d5b14ce27b0bb7d1a2bb77f02b12ab36e5f9d7e4e03a487b2988d09f" :answer-field/choices ["q-130573/selection/35ff00cbcf2a4a436b128a72e6f455ba8059d75be15bd5630c813d42a056329a" "q-130573/selection/2e2fe98a36cbe7c66ae79581c1fdd75b0f5c7f4a981eacf24dd5f5f8809f4122" "q-130573/selection/fe6d27559e5abcde73055ea85de7c50227a6e0dceca7459f59df25f54c346eaa" "q-130573/selection/6585fa59d5b14ce27b0bb7d1a2bb77f02b12ab36e5f9d7e4e03a487b2988d09f" "q-130573/selection/90f58f6c83fbc4be8de7c71ec4260b5b9f59480107489cc2ec1a33687cc9f2a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130573" :question/id #uuid "17a6bd62-d6c1-5592-8320-691a597425f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-130573" :question/problem "A math exam consists of $10$ questions, with each question containing four possible answers, where only one is the correct answer. Michael takes the exam and, for each question, he randomly chooses one of the four answers. What is the probability that Michael answers at least eight questions correctly? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of questions Michael answers correctly.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ questions on the exam\n\n- the probability of choosing a correct answer is $p=\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,\\frac{1}{4}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})}^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that Michael answers at least $8$ questions correctly. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 8) & =P(X=8)+P(X=9)+P(X=10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{8}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{8}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{10-8}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{10}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{10-10} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{8}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+10{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{9}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}+1{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{10}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.0004\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-130573/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130573"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130744/selection/7b717a10d04e3969dd181cc1afb36b332e5b837faa5b9615aa100285a4b31b22" :answer/id #uuid "e10f38d7-2ce0-516d-9f97-e5f0d0581759" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0006" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130744/selection/92b8236346025991b05982d743b7690c3d27d0c353c20acf839c899bd0323c0e" :answer/id #uuid "498c0f18-ebe6-5860-b599-0ae0ced2012f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0126" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130744/selection/e249dc1acb750d64a2c73db22b493f7fb9e8c17c63b02f8259d0bde27b0a0d39" :answer/id #uuid "ac09f856-b3ae-5e9e-a94b-becd8a4fe3f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1250" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130744/selection/4bffc8e9f33e43dcf3c7f7194d6c34678e6751f89e060de725cfa6590a54d1d9" :answer/id #uuid "655f0f31-84e0-56c3-a40d-487cc4dabc95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0327" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130744/selection/4d6a40fbbc9fe36f7d42d7bf364be182d2b837874cd60e8ff34fdc5f224fa564" :answer/id #uuid "9d323686-7afb-5570-8175-8e081c30f762" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0088" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130744/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "464ba014-0e1d-5c8d-b7d7-9473f5c629cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-130744/selection/7b717a10d04e3969dd181cc1afb36b332e5b837faa5b9615aa100285a4b31b22" :answer-field/choices ["q-130744/selection/7b717a10d04e3969dd181cc1afb36b332e5b837faa5b9615aa100285a4b31b22" "q-130744/selection/92b8236346025991b05982d743b7690c3d27d0c353c20acf839c899bd0323c0e" "q-130744/selection/e249dc1acb750d64a2c73db22b493f7fb9e8c17c63b02f8259d0bde27b0a0d39" "q-130744/selection/4bffc8e9f33e43dcf3c7f7194d6c34678e6751f89e060de725cfa6590a54d1d9" "q-130744/selection/4d6a40fbbc9fe36f7d42d7bf364be182d2b837874cd60e8ff34fdc5f224fa564"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130744" :question/id #uuid "d6929414-119d-5b75-9482-bc1d675f0f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130744" :question/problem "In a certain school, $17.5%$ of students are left-handed. In a random sample of $8$ students from this school, what is the probability that at least $6$ students are left-handed? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of left-handed students in the group.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ students in the group\n\n- the probability of a student being left-handed is $p=0.175$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.175).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(0.175)}^{x}{(1-0.175)}^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(0.175)}^{x}{(0.825)}^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least $6$ students are left-handed. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =P(X=6)+P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{6}){(0.175)}^{6}{(0.825)}^{8-6}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7}){(0.175)}^{7}{(0.825)}^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8}){(0.175)}^{8}{(0.825)}^{8-8} \\\\  & =28{(0.175)}^{6}{(0.825)}^{2}+8{(0.175)}^{7}{(0.825)}^{1}+1{(0.175)}^{8}{(0.825)}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.0006\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-130744/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130744"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130746/selection/42159cef51df3288c90517aa1f9fc8d49bf0898ede32694a695d8d5c0b58a235" :answer/id #uuid "94d7f602-ff6b-5e99-b911-d93d251b7da0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.329" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130746/selection/e148328cd517bdebaa042200a5a4a0d9eb6cd3cb62aac30eb85aca970a2ad65c" :answer/id #uuid "ae8d3aab-bd6b-544f-8011-ce93152632c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.156" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130746/selection/6143ae7fa9508c517ee7c12ea7516a335ee24d9575e27d3f2455f6b9cb42db95" :answer/id #uuid "45cf1966-f172-58ed-89dd-e819d92006d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.407" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130746/selection/48fb4afd70a5a8d8c5c01a602c7c81d88cf62dda82c22c4c69cdfef2b24d1b04" :answer/id #uuid "711ccb47-ecb8-5aed-b150-eb5bce323c3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.296" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130746/selection/ef0d9bc63b6f600ee4630224c6499b39fd9dcb6eca375922764a2f2a91b8c256" :answer/id #uuid "7cbe8223-0576-5868-b96b-4d0d4522b650" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.889" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130746/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9028d049-3d04-5c2d-bfc0-3eddbd8948ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-130746/selection/6143ae7fa9508c517ee7c12ea7516a335ee24d9575e27d3f2455f6b9cb42db95" :answer-field/choices ["q-130746/selection/42159cef51df3288c90517aa1f9fc8d49bf0898ede32694a695d8d5c0b58a235" "q-130746/selection/e148328cd517bdebaa042200a5a4a0d9eb6cd3cb62aac30eb85aca970a2ad65c" "q-130746/selection/6143ae7fa9508c517ee7c12ea7516a335ee24d9575e27d3f2455f6b9cb42db95" "q-130746/selection/48fb4afd70a5a8d8c5c01a602c7c81d88cf62dda82c22c4c69cdfef2b24d1b04" "q-130746/selection/ef0d9bc63b6f600ee4630224c6499b39fd9dcb6eca375922764a2f2a91b8c256"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130746" :question/id #uuid "95b91ca4-b34b-549c-bb1a-26c0529d749b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130746" :question/problem "A box of chocolates contains cherry creams and almond little cakes in the ratio of $2:1.$ Four chocolates are selected at random, with replacement. What is the probability of getting at most $2$ cherry creams? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of selected cherry creams.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=4$ selected chocolates\n\n- since cherry creams and almond little cakes are in the ratio of $2:1,$ the probability of selecting a cherry cream from the box is $p=\\frac{2}{3}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(4,\\frac{2}{3}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting at most $2$ cherry creams. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-2} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}+4{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}+6{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.407\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-130746/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130746"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331526/field-1/f4fccdfee7ea3ad85675fb2c01cdfd8f539e04222742e6615ea1eb49ae07cd0d" :answer/id #uuid "61733493-2bbc-5b5a-add1-f3e290e7b690" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.031" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331526/field-1/ff44475a96e3cdb16c33f11640d8315159a1bb29809b0492f22e88709d78a8c3" :answer/id #uuid "7f4d364c-df5c-5cca-b246-0e6117eeb2fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.030" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331526/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7d750b-b4ba-5ae0-8dbd-079f79dfe4f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331526/field-1/f4fccdfee7ea3ad85675fb2c01cdfd8f539e04222742e6615ea1eb49ae07cd0d" :answer-field/choices ["q-331526/field-1/f4fccdfee7ea3ad85675fb2c01cdfd8f539e04222742e6615ea1eb49ae07cd0d" "q-331526/field-1/ff44475a96e3cdb16c33f11640d8315159a1bb29809b0492f22e88709d78a8c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331526" :question/id #uuid "ea0d449c-1cdc-5c3f-99b6-765afc4040fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-331526" :question/problem "A new software program fails to run with probability $70%.$ If it is tested on $5$ computers, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that it fails on $1$ or fewer of them?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of failed runs.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ tests.\n\n- The probability of failure is $p=70%=0.7.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.7).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that $1$ or fewer of the $5$ software program runs fail. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0})(0.7)^{0}(0.3)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.7)^{1}(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =1(0.3)^{5}+5(0.7)(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.031,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331526/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331526"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-84787/selection/ea81fbbd90a79c17a9206cdb98161a6740d53a5c7fa1f0e5a86fcdabc94e355c" :answer/id #uuid "f8faa9f9-8290-55a0-8955-64262886ccd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.328" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84787/selection/28230ff17cb0388e21f0646082a1642689d46d4c6b4e24577b6117f194bdb42e" :answer/id #uuid "b4b68ac8-5fe2-5764-8d69-a2974341184f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.363" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84787/selection/d62c113922ef8098e23d42a864b163dfcafc1882e448d7f7d9e3c13e7a5a135c" :answer/id #uuid "e5c61903-0021-5a45-86b2-d1b30801a581" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.345" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84787/selection/17efb07d56390f6d21c2a8d68e49fe0a8cb4f97dbfdf90c0da49b9a9e64e6e61" :answer/id #uuid "02f5288d-2dd8-528d-b671-7eaafc2e22ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.297" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84787/selection/e61fff6720b8cafa0ea7cb10b52ecd490fe413cca04d4fe5b1582e2c57c287c1" :answer/id #uuid "16614ff7-5702-57b7-b5a8-282cb1c1c9ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.289" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84787/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6a40f4ed-ef04-5194-89c6-dcc679b33346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-84787/selection/28230ff17cb0388e21f0646082a1642689d46d4c6b4e24577b6117f194bdb42e" :answer-field/choices ["q-84787/selection/ea81fbbd90a79c17a9206cdb98161a6740d53a5c7fa1f0e5a86fcdabc94e355c" "q-84787/selection/28230ff17cb0388e21f0646082a1642689d46d4c6b4e24577b6117f194bdb42e" "q-84787/selection/d62c113922ef8098e23d42a864b163dfcafc1882e448d7f7d9e3c13e7a5a135c" "q-84787/selection/17efb07d56390f6d21c2a8d68e49fe0a8cb4f97dbfdf90c0da49b9a9e64e6e61" "q-84787/selection/e61fff6720b8cafa0ea7cb10b52ecd490fe413cca04d4fe5b1582e2c57c287c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84787" :question/id #uuid "f1fa5c5c-1256-58a4-a2e6-09fcb984e75a" :question/math-academy-id "q-84787" :question/problem "A fair coin is tossed $8$ times. What is the probability of getting at most $3$ heads? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of \"heads\" obtained when tossing a coin.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ tosses\n\n- the probability of getting a \"head\" is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., a \"head\") on at most $3$ tosses. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{1}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8-2}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{3}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8-3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8}+8(\\frac{1}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}+28{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}+56{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} \\\\  & =0.363\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-84787/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84787"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331923/field-1/a7b2b51da09797c8ff716e2381919198cc0efef9072003d034630e9d9042b4a6" :answer/id #uuid "911d3f28-c013-5abc-82de-dd20bc9fc8b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.624" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331923/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "37054962-058b-50c9-a759-c231d31ddbdc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331923/field-1/a7b2b51da09797c8ff716e2381919198cc0efef9072003d034630e9d9042b4a6" :answer-field/choices ["q-331923/field-1/a7b2b51da09797c8ff716e2381919198cc0efef9072003d034630e9d9042b4a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331923" :question/id #uuid "74ab2e3c-1d15-5ca2-8427-1d2761d5ef2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331923" :question/problem "A text message has a $20%$ chance of being flagged as spam by a filter. Suppose $10$ messages are randomly selected. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at least $2$ are flagged as spam?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of messages flagged as spam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ messages.\n\n- The probability of a message being flagged as spam is $p=20%=0.20.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.20).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(1-0.20)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(0.80)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of at least $2$ messages being flagged. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.20)^{0}(0.80)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.20)^{1}(0.80)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.80)^{10}+10(0.20)(0.80)^{9}] \\\\  & =1-0.375\\,809… \\\\  & ≈0.624,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331923/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331923"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-84805/selection/f34957a00d5f57ae5de730067aea24aee9ed87a056d36898bd62c3fcf72f7660" :answer/id #uuid "19afb6ee-db01-54c4-a459-17c3dd9a485c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.648" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84805/selection/ed4cc11a57935a185937f87a8049febc040c5f06612066225e9871b2bcd4edd9" :answer/id #uuid "fc01552c-81de-52fd-b74b-cb2289593700" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.351" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84805/selection/b073257cffa13527462e11c98827dc1a82f456f282a23d06d13cb217b6b3c1f7" :answer/id #uuid "c1b7d9c4-b958-52e8-b7e9-6495219ebd79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.602" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84805/selection/58f567c7ea84b4a7638b3c768b6b87b9ac7e5863db5c04034b7513a7c47302ba" :answer/id #uuid "5b5841bf-c4f0-549f-8e63-0273619d81b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.592" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84805/selection/560db54cf6e091fad0bbe0fc524059441e81c9e7e4db7780a9a887d797a41323" :answer/id #uuid "ccaaf3e0-1ff6-51ff-8e17-e0ba15e8e884" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.398" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84805/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6fbf6e6c-ce8e-583a-ad06-5d47fdbca07f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-84805/selection/b073257cffa13527462e11c98827dc1a82f456f282a23d06d13cb217b6b3c1f7" :answer-field/choices ["q-84805/selection/f34957a00d5f57ae5de730067aea24aee9ed87a056d36898bd62c3fcf72f7660" "q-84805/selection/ed4cc11a57935a185937f87a8049febc040c5f06612066225e9871b2bcd4edd9" "q-84805/selection/b073257cffa13527462e11c98827dc1a82f456f282a23d06d13cb217b6b3c1f7" "q-84805/selection/58f567c7ea84b4a7638b3c768b6b87b9ac7e5863db5c04034b7513a7c47302ba" "q-84805/selection/560db54cf6e091fad0bbe0fc524059441e81c9e7e4db7780a9a887d797a41323"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84805" :question/id #uuid "e6406e18-da76-59f2-961a-f419b2168ca4" :question/math-academy-id "q-84805" :question/problem "A party decorator knows that one out of every five balloons he blows up pops right away. If the decorator blows up $15$ balloons, what is the probability that more than $2$ balloons pop? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of balloons that pop.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=15$ balloons\n\n- the probability that one balloon pops is $p=\\frac{1}{5}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(15,\\frac{1}{5}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{x}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})}^{15-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{x}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting at least $3$ balloons that pop. This corresponds to $P(X>2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X>2)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{0}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{0}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{1}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{1}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{2}){(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15-2}] \\\\  & =1-[{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{15}+15(\\frac{1}{5}){(\\frac{4}{5})}^{14}+105{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{13}] \\\\  & =1-0.398\\,023 \\\\  & =0.602\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-84805/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84805"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-130878/selection/11d7921a0ad0692696a06cabe27f65f5ad55b7c347e07d10b3ac08e5c1d96479" :answer/id #uuid "b0db495a-ff26-5066-9556-1523fb669171" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.382" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130878/selection/c474092ddc28caa16ab68fc15a435c31216ec4297d564eea44b9c30249d95c5c" :answer/id #uuid "72cce0ad-05cb-5ba1-9c23-29af52010c62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.791" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130878/selection/08446f6a60f50dd14b4f7332cf9dd4cb47b86d23235afb1c8f57e33f4ea713cd" :answer/id #uuid "3dca2c2b-28bb-569f-b2cd-24713de0112d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.233" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130878/selection/aecec68d504d9ac6b0e23c52a24521682f047a3bcf166551365f46daade64b72" :answer/id #uuid "3ffd6064-d2c4-5308-a7c3-0c0449b9c101" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.851" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130878/selection/7d047d88f6c3490e4e42c7814a2ff28c51a094aef8596f6fb77873d5fde1c80c" :answer/id #uuid "94df3645-4bfd-5634-a1a6-c349d2f52e6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.687" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-130878/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "63aeb33c-da83-507c-af73-b301384365f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-130878/selection/aecec68d504d9ac6b0e23c52a24521682f047a3bcf166551365f46daade64b72" :answer-field/choices ["q-130878/selection/11d7921a0ad0692696a06cabe27f65f5ad55b7c347e07d10b3ac08e5c1d96479" "q-130878/selection/c474092ddc28caa16ab68fc15a435c31216ec4297d564eea44b9c30249d95c5c" "q-130878/selection/08446f6a60f50dd14b4f7332cf9dd4cb47b86d23235afb1c8f57e33f4ea713cd" "q-130878/selection/aecec68d504d9ac6b0e23c52a24521682f047a3bcf166551365f46daade64b72" "q-130878/selection/7d047d88f6c3490e4e42c7814a2ff28c51a094aef8596f6fb77873d5fde1c80c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-130878" :question/id #uuid "4fcb80d4-6b8d-54f6-8fb8-39ba159a3c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-130878" :question/problem "A basketball player has a $70%$ success rate in shooting from the free throw line during a season. The player has ten shots from the free throw line in one match. What is the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of successful free throws.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ shots from the free throw line in the match\n\n- the probability that the player is successful from the free throw line is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 8).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 8)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=8)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8) & =1-P(X>8) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=9)+P(X=10)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10}] \\\\  & =1-[10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{1}+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0}] \\\\  & =1-0.149\\,308 \\\\  & ≈0.851\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-130878/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-130878"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-84804/selection/dfb380c9921a182c69d74beeab489ea09740b68b2eca1a8a4be89f6dd87fdffb" :answer/id #uuid "3d19da8d-38bf-5ec8-acc7-9c09603f546d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.983" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84804/selection/023aedb0b70b864f73365d3484abf7c8b1059bb1b00bdbb2100906d6f3cdfa06" :answer/id #uuid "cca28471-c981-5a78-b4a8-b2dead0117b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.916" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84804/selection/77edad3689119f2a9492cfd861e97307098cbc4d8e8547959a8635a949908c42" :answer/id #uuid "46944fb0-9059-5144-b521-ca81f7741f8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.020" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84804/selection/5311bf5e6f3adb79c5b77d49072c79619c277d306268d4e33da6a1c59c058aa2" :answer/id #uuid "ad80dfef-a9d1-5359-96a0-d8f2d2e00e42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.979" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84804/selection/f6e80b1b710cee3e28dd6801d0effecdde0d0eb77ac7c9456c2233340b797410" :answer/id #uuid "1f6f2061-73ec-5645-8430-3f4e679efc3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.943" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84804/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "69ea4569-9a35-5df7-9c79-eb9e58857090" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-84804/selection/5311bf5e6f3adb79c5b77d49072c79619c277d306268d4e33da6a1c59c058aa2" :answer-field/choices ["q-84804/selection/dfb380c9921a182c69d74beeab489ea09740b68b2eca1a8a4be89f6dd87fdffb" "q-84804/selection/023aedb0b70b864f73365d3484abf7c8b1059bb1b00bdbb2100906d6f3cdfa06" "q-84804/selection/77edad3689119f2a9492cfd861e97307098cbc4d8e8547959a8635a949908c42" "q-84804/selection/5311bf5e6f3adb79c5b77d49072c79619c277d306268d4e33da6a1c59c058aa2" "q-84804/selection/f6e80b1b710cee3e28dd6801d0effecdde0d0eb77ac7c9456c2233340b797410"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84804" :question/id #uuid "71e8d013-8b88-5657-bbca-2113b716fcd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-84804" :question/problem "A painter at an exhibition has a $35%$ chance of selling each of his works of art. What is the probability that when exhibiting $14$ works of art, the painter sells $2$ or more? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of works of art sold.\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=14$ works of art\n\n- the probability of selling each work is $p=35%=0.35$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(14,0.35).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(1-0.35)^{14-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(0.65)^{14-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the painter sells $2$ or more works of art. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=14)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{0})(0.35)^{0}(0.65)^{14-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{1})(0.35)^{1}(0.65)^{14-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.65)^{14}+14(0.35)(0.65)^{13}] \\\\  & =1-0.020\\,519 \\\\  & =0.979\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-84804/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84804"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-84153"] :question/problem "A golfer has a $10%$ chance of winning each tournament they enter. If the golfer enters $20$ tournaments, what is the probability that they win at least $2$ tournaments? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of tournaments that the golfer wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=10%=0.1$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.1).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.1)^{x}(1-0.1)^{20-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{x})(0.1)^{x}(0.9)^{20-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the golfer wins at least $2$ tournaments. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=20)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{0})(0.1)^{0}(0.9)^{20-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{1})(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{20-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.9)^{20}+20(0.1)(0.9)^{19}] \\\\  & =1-0.391\\,747 \\\\  & =0.608\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7394"] :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The ages of $20$ college professors\n\n- The number of questions answered correctly by a student in a $12$-question exam\n\n- The heights of $10$ elementary school children" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of questions answered in a $12$- question exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $12$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The heights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7392"] :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of students that passed the exam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ students\n\n- the probability that each student passed the exam is $p=75\\%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(5,0.75).$ We want to find the probability that $x=3$ of the students passed the exam.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1-0.75)^{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!}(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =0.264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7393"] :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ rolls\n\n- the probability of rolling an even number on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{3}{6} = 0.5\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(8,0.5).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1-0.5)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of rolling an even number at least $7$ times. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 7) & =P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.5)^{8}(0.5)^{8-8} \\\\  & =8(0.5)^{7}(0.5)+1(0.5)^{8} \\\\  & =0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7444"] :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the player wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=40\\%=0.4$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(12,0.4).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{12-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{12-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player wins at least 2 of the games. This corresponds to $P(X \\geq 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2) = P(X=2) + P(X=3) + \\ldots + P(X=12)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{12-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{12-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.6)^{12}+12(0.4)(0.6)^{11}] \\\\  & =1-0.019\\,591 \\\\  & =0.980\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
